// Origin Story of the Shakti Peethas:
// The Shakti Peethas are sacred shrines associated with the story of Goddess Sati, the first wife
// of Lord Shiva. Sati was the daughter of Daksha Prajapati. Daksha organized a grand yajna
// (fire sacrifice) but deliberately did not invite Shiva, whom he considered beneath his dignity.
// Sati, despite Shiva's warnings, insisted on attending. At the yajna, Daksha publicly insulted
// Shiva in front of everyone. Unable to bear the humiliation of her husband, Sati immolated
// herself in the yajna fire.
//
// When Shiva learned of Sati's death, he was consumed with inconsolable grief. He picked up
// Sati's body and began wandering through the universe, performing the grief-stricken Tandava
// dance. The universe trembled at Shiva's grief and anger. To restore cosmic order, Lord Vishnu
// used his Sudarshana Chakra (divine disc) to dismember Sati's body as Shiva carried it.
// Different accounts say the body was cut into 51 or 108 pieces, each piece falling to the earth
// at a different location. Where each piece fell, the earth became sacred, and a Shakti Peetha
// (abode of Shakti/Goddess) was established. Each Peetha has a presiding goddess (a form of Sati/
// Parvati) and a Bhairava (a fierce form of Shiva) who guards the goddess.

