    story:
      'Kamakhya is considered the most powerful of all the Shakti Peethas. When Vishnu\'s Sudarshana Chakra dismembered Sati\'s body, her yoni (womb/genitalia) fell on the Nilachal Hill in Guwahati. This is the most intimate and powerful part of the divine feminine, and its falling here made Kamakhya the seat of the greatest feminine power in the universe.\n\nThe goddess here is known as Kamakhya – the Goddess of Desire and Love (Kama = desire, Aksha = eyes; or from Kamakya = she who grants desires). She is associated with Tantra and is worshipped by Tantric practitioners from all over India. The goddess does not have an anthropomorphic idol – she is worshipped in the form of a yoni-shaped rock face that is permanently moistened by an underground spring. This spring is considered the menstrual blood of the goddess, and the Ambubachi Mela celebrated annually when the goddess is believed to menstruate is one of the most extraordinary Hindu festivals in India.\n\nThe Ambubachi Mela (held in June) draws hundreds of thousands of Tantric sadhus, aghoris, and devotees. During these three days, the temple is closed, symbolizing the goddess\'s menstrual period. After the three days, the temple reopens and pilgrims receive prasad in the form of red cloth, symbolizing the menstrual blood of the goddess. This practice, far from being taboo, celebrates the creative power of the feminine principle.\n\nThe current temple on Nilachal Hill was built in 1565 CE by the Ahom king Nara Narayan on the ruins of an older temple that was destroyed by Muslim invaders in the 16th century. The temple is built in a distinctive beehive-shaped (cruciform) style unique to Assamese architecture. The temple complex also includes shrines to the ten Mahavidyas – the ten powerful forms of the Goddess – making it a complete Shakta pilgrimage center.',
