      'The Kalighat temple in Kolkata is one of the most famous temples in India, housing one of the 51 Shakti Peethas where the toes of Sati\'s right foot fell. This is the temple that gave Kolkata (formerly Calcutta) its name – Kali-ghat (Steps/Ghat of Kali) evolved into Calcutta and then Kolkata.\n\nThe Kalighat temple has a particularly fierce form of Kali as its presiding deity. The idol of Goddess Kali at Kalighat is unusual and striking – it is made of black stone with a large tongue extended outward (as Kali is depicted), with long arms, wide eyes, and a fierce expression. Every day, thousands of devotees wait in long queues for a glimpse of the goddess. The darshan is extremely brief but considered immensely powerful.\n\nThe history of the Kalighat temple as a formal structure goes back to 1809 CE when the current temple was constructed. However, it is believed that a small shrine existed at this location for centuries before. The famous Indian saint Sri Ramakrishna Paramahamsa, whose disciple was Swami Vivekananda, was a priest at the nearby Dakshineswar Kali temple and is closely associated with the Kalighat tradition of Kali worship. Animal sacrifice (primarily goats) is traditionally offered at Kalighat and continues to be practiced.\n\nThe temple is located on the banks of the Adi Ganga, a smaller distributary of the Ganga. The spiritual tradition holds that the Adi Ganga flows past the feet of Goddess Kali at Kalighat, sanctifying it. The idol is bathed in Ganga water and the goddess is offered elaborate bhog (food offerings) including fish, which are uniquely accepted here.',
