    story:
      'The Jwalamukhi temple in Kangra district of Himachal Pradesh is a uniquely miraculous Shakti Peetha where Sati\'s tongue fell. Here, the goddess manifests not in an anthropomorphic idol but as eternal natural flames that burn from the earth without any fuel or oil. These nine flames (representing the nine forms of the goddess) have been burning continuously for thousands of years from natural methane gas seeping through cracks in the rock. The central flame is considered the tongue of Sati herself.\n\nThe nine flames represent the nine forms of the goddess: Mahakali, Unpurna, Chandi, Hinglaj, Bindhya Basni, Mahalakshmi, Saraswati, Ambika, and Anjana. The Mughal Emperor Akbar, fascinated by this miracle, tried to extinguish the flames with water from a canal he ordered built specifically for this purpose. The flames continued to burn undeterred. Impressed by this divine power, Akbar reportedly offered a golden canopy (chatri) to the goddess. The story says the goddess transformed the gold canopy into a different metal (some say zinc or antimony) to show that she does not need material offerings.\n\nThe Jwalamukhi temple has been a pilgrimage site for thousands of years. The Mahabharata mentions this as "Jwaladevata" (Fire Goddess) shrine. The Pandavas are said to have visited here during their time of exile. The temple was destroyed by Muslim invaders in medieval times but rebuilt. Raja Bhumi Chand of Kangra built the current temple structure in 1835 CE.\n\nThe town of Jwalamukhi is in the Beas River valley of Himachal Pradesh, flanked by the Dhauladhar mountain range. The approach to the temple through the mountain scenery is spectacular. The nine flames are visible inside the temple, some licking the surface of a stone slab, some coming from a tank filled with water (the water remains cool despite the flames). This combination of fire and water is considered a divine miracle.',
