      'The Naina Devi temple is perched on a hilltop (about 1,177 meters above sea level) near Bilaspur in Himachal Pradesh. According to the Shakti Peetha tradition, the eyes (naina) of Goddess Sati fell here, giving the goddess her name Naina Devi (Goddess of Eyes). The eyes represent the vision of the divine – the power to see all things clearly, to grant sight to the blind, and to bless devotees with spiritual vision.\n\nThe Naina Devi temple overlooks the Gobind Sagar Lake, the vast reservoir created by the Bhakra Dam on the Sutlej River. The combination of the hilltop temple and the expansive lake below creates a breathtaking visual panorama. The original town of Naina Devi was submerged when the Gobind Sagar reservoir was created, and the temple was relocated to the nearby hill where it stands today.\n\nThe story of Naina Devi is also intertwined with a local legend about a young cowherd boy (gopala) who was led by his cows to a particular spot every day. He found that a white cow\'s milk dropped on a stone at that spot. He reported this to the local ruler, who discovered the stone to be a Shakti Peetha and had a temple constructed. This legend is common to many Shakti Peethas, suggesting a motif of the goddess being "rediscovered" in each era.\n\nThe Naina Devi temple is extremely popular among devotees from Punjab, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh, and beyond. The Navratri festivals (both Chaitra and Ashwin Navratri) draw enormous crowds. An aerial ropeway (cable car) connects the base to the hilltop temple, making the pilgrimage accessible to those unable to climb the 1.5-km foot path.',
