      'The Katyayani Shakti Peetha at Vrindavan is where Sati\'s hair (kesh/locks) is believed to have fallen. The goddess Katyayani is the sixth form among the Navadurgas (nine forms of Durga) and is born from the fury of all the gods to slay the demon Mahishasura. She is named after the sage Katyayana, in whose hermitage she was manifested, or alternatively, the "daughter of Katya" (one of the names of the sage Maharishi Katyayana).\n\nThe connection of Katyayani Devi with Vrindavan, the sacred land of Krishna, creates a beautiful interweaving of Shakta and Vaishnava traditions. According to the Bhagavata Purana, the Gopis (cowherd women) of Vrindavan observed the Katyayani Vrata (a month-long vow) during the Margashirsha month, worshipping Katyayani to obtain Lord Krishna as their husband. They would bathe in the Yamuna before sunrise, make a clay image of Katyayani, worship her with flowers, incense, and sandalwood paste, and pray for Krishna as their Lord. This ancient tradition connects the goddess directly to the greatest devotion story in Vaishnavism.\n\nThe Katyayani Shaktipeeth at Vrindavan is therefore uniquely sacred as a confluence of Shakta and Vaishnava streams of worship. The temple is located in the forests of Vrindavan, in the same landscape where Krishna spent his divine childhood. The sacred Vrindavan forest (Braj) is itself considered the embodiment of Radha-Krishna\'s divine love, and the goddess Katyayani is the divine power that grants devotees union with their ultimate beloved.',
