    story:
      'Hinglaj Mata is considered one of the most powerful Shakti Peethas, where the crown of Sati\'s head (brahmarandhra – the crown of the skull, the highest chakra) fell. Although it is located in Balochistan, Pakistan (making it inaccessible to many Indian pilgrims), Hinglaj remains deeply revered in Indian religious consciousness as one of the 51 Shakti Peethas and is listed among the most significant.\n\nThe Hinglaj shrine is unique in that it is not a man-made temple but a natural cave where the goddess is worshipped. Inside the cave is a deposit of ochre-colored material (possibly a natural mineral deposit) that is worshipped as the form of the goddess. The site is remarkable for being in the Makran coastal desert of Balochistan, a harsh, arid landscape that heightens the sense of the goddess\'s divine power manifesting in a most unlikely environment.\n\nThe Hinglaj Yatra (pilgrimage) is held annually in April and draws hundreds of thousands of devotees from all over Pakistan – including both Hindus and Muslims. The local Muslims call her "Nani Mandir" and venerate her as well. The yatra is a 50 km journey through the harsh desert, and its continuation in Pakistan to this day is a testament to the enduring power of the goddess tradition across religious boundaries.\n\nFor Indian devotees, Hinglaj holds a special place in the 51 Shakti Peethas list even though it is geographically inaccessible. Some devotees perform symbolic pilgrimages or create replicas. The other Hinglaj temples that have been established in India (notably in Porbandar, Gujarat) serve as surrogate pilgrimage sites for devotees who cannot travel to Pakistan.',
